---
import Prose from '../layouts/Prose.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---

<Prose
  title="Editorial policy"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} researches, reviews, corrects and funds its guides.`}
  lede="How we decide what to publish, how we check it, and what advertising is and isn't allowed to influence."
  updated="8 August 2026"
>
  <h2>What we publish</h2>
  <p>
    We cover problems that <em>recur</em>: the drain that slows down every
    autumn, the toilet that starts running after a year, the phone that fills up
    every six months. A good {SITE.name} topic has three properties — it happens
    to lots of people, it has a physical mechanism that can be drawn, and it can
    be fixed by a careful non-expert with ordinary tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    We turn a topic down when the fix is genuinely dangerous, when it varies so
    much by region or model that a single animation would mislead, or when the
    honest answer is "call someone".
  </p>

  <h2>How a guide is researched</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Mechanism first.</strong> Before writing a single step we work out
      why the thing fails. If we can't state the cause in one sentence, we don't
      understand it well enough to draw it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cross-check against primary sources.</strong> Manufacturer
      instructions, published standards and building or plumbing codes take
      priority over other how-to sites. Where regions differ (fitting sizes,
      voltages, terminology) we say so rather than quietly picking one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Storyboard, then draw.</strong> The fix is broken into four to six
      states. Each state has to be understandable as a still frame. If a step
      only makes sense in motion, it's usually two steps.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Text and animation are written together.</strong> The written
      steps are not a summary written afterwards — they are the same script. If
      one changes, both change.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Accuracy and limits</h2>
  <p>
    Every guide states what it assumes (typical fixtures, common models) and
    where it stops. Guides that touch on safety carry an explicit "stop here"
    line. We would rather lose a reader to a plumber than keep one in a flooded
    bathroom.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not publish guides that help people bypass safety devices, defeat
    copyright or licensing protection, or circumvent security measures.
  </p>

  <h2>Review and updates</h2>
  <p>
    Guides carry a published date and, once reviewed, a "last reviewed" date. We
    re-check guides when a reader reports a problem, when a referenced standard
    or app interface changes, and otherwise on a rolling basis. Device guides
    age fastest and get looked at most often.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If we get something wrong, we fix it in place — animation and text together
    — and update the review date. Substantive corrections (ones that change what
    you should actually do) are noted at the foot of the guide. Send corrections
    to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> with "Correction" in the
    subject line.
  </p>

  <h2>Use of AI tools</h2>
  <p>
    We use software tooling, including AI assistance, to help draft and to
    generate the vector artwork behind our animations. Nothing is published
    unreviewed: a human decides the mechanism, checks every claim against
    sources, and signs off the final animation and text. Errors are ours, not a
    tool's, and we treat them that way.
  </p>

  <h2>How we're funded, and the line we don't cross</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is funded by display advertising. Ad slots are clearly labelled
    and separated from content so you always know which is which.
  </p>
  <p>Advertising does not influence:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>which problems we choose to cover;</li>
    <li>which product or method a guide recommends;</li>
    <li>whether a guide tells you to stop and hire a professional.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Advertisers get no advance sight of guides and no right of review. If we
    ever publish sponsored or affiliate content, it will be labelled as such at
    the top of the page, before you read a word of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence from manufacturers</h2>
  <p>
    We are not affiliated with any manufacturer whose products appear in guides.
    Product names are used descriptively so you can identify the part in your
    own home.
  </p>
</Prose>
